{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/506451-question-regarding-dodging-combat","id":506451,"title":"Question regarding dodging & combat","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-07T00:41:24-07:00","id":506451,"title":"Question regarding dodging & combat","content":"Question:<br /> <br /> When you decide to forego all other actions that round and dodge, do you still get to use Defense to reduce any leftover damage from the dodge? That is, if you opponent had 6 successes and you had a defense 2 and dodged (rolled four successes in this case), would you take no damage, or two?<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2798-ordo-septenarius","name":"Ordo Septenarius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T01:32:20-07:00","id":506526,"title":"","content":"Um.<br /> <br /> I'm not exactly sure where you're coming from with this. From the phrasing it sounds like you're asking about the 2e rules, not the 1e rules (since you're talking about rolling Dodge).<br /> <br /> In either edition Dodge completely replaces normal Defense though.<br /> <br /> It feels like you might be confusing how armor works when mixed with Dodge instead of Defense.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T03:59:45-07:00","id":506593,"title":"","content":"In 1e, dodge doubles the defense, but it subtracts the number from the dice pool of the attacker. The dodge action in 2e replaces the basic defense and the successes remove successes from the attackers attack roll. Assuming you are running 2e, the character takes 2 damage in your example (assuming the attacker is using a 0 damage weapon, because it is 2 + the damage of the weapon).<br /> <br /> It should be noted that while closely scanning 1e WoD and the God Machine rule update result in a bit of a contradictory rule set. The God Machine booklet mentions to use 1e WoD rules unless otherwise stated. No mention is made about defense working differently, but at the same time the God Machine rules state it is attack vs. defense and the text in the damage section also implies defense is subtracted from damage (as if it is an opposed check removing successes). Nowhere is it outright stated that defense is a penalty applied to number of successess. Which raises the question: is defense applied as a penalty to the attacker's dice pool in 2e or it is removing successes from the attacker's roll?<br /> <br /> Mind you, I am not entirely sure what the impact would be of the difference... I suspect removing successes is usually better. I do wonder whether dodging is worth it in 2e because chances are significant that you end up with less successes than your defense score, especially if you have a low defense score to begin with. Mind you, dodge is way too effective in 1e <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4560-madfox11","name":"Madfox11","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T08:53:28-07:00","id":506900,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Madfox11</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/506451-question-regarding-dodging-combat?p=506593#post506593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In 1e, dodge doubles the defense, but it subtracts the number from the dice pool of the attacker. The dodge action in 2e replaces the basic defense and the successes remove successes from the attackers attack roll. Assuming you are running 2e, the character takes 2 damage in your example (assuming the attacker is using a 0 damage weapon, because it is 2 + the damage of the weapon).<br /> <br /> It should be noted that while closely scanning 1e WoD and the God Machine rule update result in a bit of a contradictory rule set. The God Machine booklet mentions to use 1e WoD rules unless otherwise stated. No mention is made about defense working differently, but at the same time the God Machine rules state it is attack vs. defense and the text in the damage section also implies defense is subtracted from damage (as if it is an opposed check removing successes). Nowhere is it outright stated that defense is a penalty applied to number of successess. Which raises the question: is defense applied as a penalty to the attacker's dice pool in 2e or it is removing successes from the attacker's roll?<br /> <br /> Mind you, I am not entirely sure what the impact would be of the difference... I suspect removing successes is usually better. I do wonder whether dodging is worth it in 2e because chances are significant that you end up with less successes than your defense score, especially if you have a low defense score to begin with. Mind you, dodge is way too effective in 1e <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Good, it sounds like I was playing it right. <br /> <br /> Re: defenses, we have been removing the dice from the successes, not removing it from the attacker's dice pool.<br /> <br /> Thanks again, everyone, for your responses.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2798-ordo-septenarius","name":"Ordo Septenarius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T10:56:20-07:00","id":506984,"title":"","content":"Erm no. You're doing it wrong.<br /> <br /> Defense is a penalty against the attacker's dice pool. It does not apply against successes. Defense only subtracts successes if you use the Dodge maneuver to roll instead of act as a penalty:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GMC Update page 197</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character\u2019s Defense is normally subtracted from any attack dice pools where it applies. If she chooses to Dodge, the defender rolls her Defense as a dice pool against each attack. Each success reduces the attacker\u2019s successes by one. If the attacker is reduced to zero successes, the attack does nothing. If the attacker has successes remaining, add any weapon modifier to the number of successes to determine how many points of Health the target loses. All weapons deal lethal damage.<br /> ...<br /> Dodge: Double Defense, roll as a dice pool with each success subtracting one from the attacker\u2019s successes.</div> </div> </div> </div>The phrase on page 198 is not contradictory, just poorly written (the writer of the combat section of the GMCU seems to no treat &quot;Defense&quot; as &quot; Defense or Dodge.&quot;).<br /> <br /> VtR 2e, and WtF 2e have much more clear phrasing, that hopefully the new WOD full 2e core book will address.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T12:02:32-07:00","id":507079,"title":"","content":"Great. Thank you, Heavy Arms.<br /> <br /> So, Def is subtracted from dice pool, not subtracted from defenses.<br /> <br /> Dodge is rolled, with successes subtracted from the attacker's successes. <br /> <br /> Makes more sense now.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2798-ordo-septenarius","name":"Ordo Septenarius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}